{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Patchset 2 took Verified -1: the module-size gate. My reasoned marker on _purge_committed_reconciler_locks pushed rebar_reconciler/__main__.py from 798 to 804 LOC, over the locked 800 cap. Not environmental, so no recheck. Operator authorized splitting the file (verified: not in any open ticket's file_impact, not covered by an RP epic).\n\nSEAM CHOSEN — the pass-lock lifecycle, extracted whole to src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_pass_lock_lifecycle.py: _lock_steal_enabled (steal kill-switch), _resolve_held_lock (steal / re-acquire resolution), _purge_committed_reconciler_locks + _LEGACY_LOCK_FILES (legacy lock-file migration), _acquire_or_adopt_pass_lock. One existing call-graph cluster, not a mechanical slice — _post_pause_preflight consults _lock_steal_enabled, whose answer decides whether _resolve_held_lock runs, whose returned OID _acquire_or_adopt_pass_lock adopts. It also matches how the package is already laid out (_advisory_lock.py = ref backend, _ref_lock.py = CAS/lease primitives, _heartbeat.py = lease renewal, _preflight.py = visibility guard); the orchestrator-side lock sequencing was the piece still inlined in __main__.\n\nSIZES: __main__.py 804 -> 704 (below the 720-LOC band the gate itself warns at, so real headroom, not another 798); _pass_lock_lifecycle.py 133. Under the 200-500 target on purpose: that IS the cluster, and padding it with unrelated functions to reach 200 would be the mechanical carving the policy forbids — the binding constraint is the 100-LOC floor. .github/module-size-limit.txt untouched.\n\n_post_pause_preflight deliberately stayed in __main__: it returns a _Disposition, and _Disposition / PassResult / _finish_disposition are the module's shared result vocabulary (used by the pass-execution path too, and by _bridge_runner.py:152 + tests/scripts/test_run_reconcile_bridge_heldout.py:201), so moving them into the lock module would invert the dependency.\n\nPATCH TARGETS PRESERVED: __main__ imports the four moved callables into its own namespace, so main() still reads them as module globals. Verified via grep-as-string, not just Serena — the suite patches them by name: patch.object(main_mod, '_purge_committed_reconciler_locks') in test_bridge_request_heldout.py:40 and test_bridge_selection_heldout.py:66, monkeypatch.setattr in test_reconciler_last_pass_heldout.py:101 and test_bridge_status.py:165, main_mod._lock_steal_enabled / _resolve_held_lock in test_lock_steal.py, main_mod._purge_committed_reconciler_locks in test_advisory_lock_ref_backend.py. _LEGACY_LOCK_FILES is NOT re-exported (nothing outside the moved code referenced it).\n\ndocs/env-vars.md regenerated: gen_env_registry.py records the file a var is read from, and REBAR_RECONCILER_LOCK_STEAL now lives in _pass_lock_lifecycle.py. That generator-staleness test was the only other local failure the move caused.\n\nNice side effect: the split is an end-to-end check of R3 itself. The purge function's 5 seam-composition hits followed it into the new module, still sanctioned by the marker that travelled with the function, and _pass_lock_lifecycle is NOT in R3's sanctioned entry set — so the rule still applies there rather than being silently switched off by the move.\n\nGates after the split: make lint exit 0 (complexity ratchet active=49 new=0 increased=0), make typecheck exit 0 over 569 files, check_raw_git_writes.py exit 0, 3018 tests passed across tests/unit/rebar_reconciler + the lint tests + the bridge heldout script tests. Pushed as patchset 3 of Gerrit 1916 (commit 26b000d3c7); the push outdated the prior LLM-Review +1, so both votes are re-running."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787176615974287001,"uuid":"d8a5e7ce-f31d-495d-85ac-042bc29074d2"}